{"content_hash": "12986e8fa256d0a1", "version_id": 464, "article_id": 47, "level_code": "en_b2", "lang": "en", "level": 4, "level_label": "B2 中高级", "title": "Flock cuts 270 jobs as backlash against AI cameras grows", "title_zh": "Flock裁员270人，AI摄像头反对声浪高涨", "has_translation": true, "topic": "tech", "source": "guardian_tech", "source_url": "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/oct/09/flock-job-cuts", "image": {"type": "photo", "url": "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1562739974-e15519c03d87?ixid=M3wxMDk3Mzk1fDB8MXxzZWFyY2h8OHx8c3VydmVpbGxhbmNlJTIwc3RhcnR1cCUyMGZsb2NrfGVufDB8MHx8fDE3OTE2NjU4NTR8MA&ixlib=rb-4.1.0&w=1600&q=75&fm=jpg&fit=max", "provider": "unsplash", "credit": "Photo by Kaspars Eglitis on Unsplash", "author": "Kaspars Eglitis", "credit_url": "https://unsplash.com/@kasparseglitis?utm_source=daily-english-news&utm_medium=referral", "source": "Unsplash", "source_url": "https://unsplash.com/photos/bird-on-white-cctv-camera-BCMCMuTISio?utm_source=daily-english-news&utm_medium=referral", "fallback": {"type": "gradient", "from": "hsl(321, 42%, 62%)", "to": "hsl(359, 46%, 48%)", "label": "TECH", "seed": "9651cefffeca"}}, "published_date": "2026-10-09", "word_count": 197, "reading_minutes": 1.0, "lead": "Flock Safety, a company that makes AI-powered surveillance cameras, is cutting about 270 jobs. The layoffs come as more communities and lawmakers push back against its technology.", "lead_zh": "生产AI监控摄像头的Flock Safety公司正在裁减约270个工作岗位。此次裁员之际，越来越多的社区和议员开始反对其技术。", "preview": "Flock Safety plans to cut about 18% of its employees, or roughly 270 people, according to sources with direct knowledge of the plans. The la", "preview_zh": "据直接了解计划的消息人士称，Flock Safety计划裁员约18%，即大约270名员工。此次裁员是在一项自愿离职买断计划之后进行的，受影响的员工将在月底离开。该公司约有1500名员工，拒绝置评。", "has_audio": true, "audio": {"id": 451, "url": "https://pub-aba43a6fb1db4dc08fede1dbc81f3241.r2.dev/audio/47/en_b2.mp3", "duration": 94.662, "size_bytes": 571824, "engine": "edge-tts", "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural", "word_count": 231, "has_timeline": true}, "detail_url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@fc48a26/content/data/versions/464.json", "paragraphs": ["Flock Safety plans to cut about 18% of its employees, or roughly 270 people, according to sources with direct knowledge of the plans. The layoffs follow a voluntary buyout program, and affected employees will leave at the end of the month. The company, which has about 1,500 workers, declined to comment.", "Flock, based in Atlanta and founded in 2017, has grown quickly in recent years. It provides about 120,000 AI-powered cameras and license-plate readers across 49 states, serving more than 4,800 law enforcement agencies and nearly 1,000 businesses. The company says its technology helps solve crimes.", "But Flock faces growing scrutiny from regulators, lawmakers, and privacy advocates. A recent Reuters/Ipsos poll found that 38% of Americans support using Flock cameras in their communities, while 47% oppose them. In September, Florida banned automated license plate readers from state highways, citing privacy risks.", "The company also faces legal challenges, including a privacy lawsuit in Virginia that claims its cameras amount to warrantless surveillance. Home Depot investors recently demanded reviews of the retailer's partnerships with surveillance vendors after reports that Flock's data was used in immigration enforcement. Flock has raised over $950 million from investors, valuing it at $7.5 billion."], "paragraphs_zh": ["据直接了解计划的消息人士称，Flock Safety计划裁员约18%，即大约270名员工。此次裁员是在一项自愿离职买断计划之后进行的，受影响的员工将在月底离开。该公司约有1500名员工，拒绝置评。", "Flock总部位于亚特兰大，成立于2017年，近年来发展迅速。它在49个州提供约12万台AI摄像头和车牌识别器，服务于4800多个执法机构和近1000家企业。该公司表示其技术有助于破案。", "但Flock正面临监管机构、议员和隐私倡导者日益严格的审查。路透社/益普索最近的一项民调发现，38%的美国人支持在其社区使用Flock摄像头，而47%的人反对。9月，佛罗里达州以隐私风险为由，禁止在州高速公路上使用自动车牌识别器。", "该公司还面临法律挑战，包括弗吉尼亚州的一起隐私诉讼，指控其摄像头的大规模部署构成无证监视。家得宝的投资者最近要求审查该零售商与监控供应商的合作关系，此前有报道称Flock的数据被用于移民执法。Flock已从投资者那里筹集了超过9.5亿美元，估值达75亿美元。"], "body": "Flock Safety plans to cut about 18% of its employees, or roughly 270 people, according to sources with direct knowledge of the plans. The layoffs follow a voluntary buyout program, and affected employees will leave at the end of the month. The company, which has about 1,500 workers, declined to comment.\n\nFlock, based in Atlanta and founded in 2017, has grown quickly in recent years. It provides about 120,000 AI-powered cameras and license-plate readers across 49 states, serving more than 4,800 law enforcement agencies and nearly 1,000 businesses. The company says its technology helps solve crimes.\n\nBut Flock faces growing scrutiny from regulators, lawmakers, and privacy advocates. A recent Reuters/Ipsos poll found that 38% of Americans support using Flock cameras in their communities, while 47% oppose them. In September, Florida banned automated license plate readers from state highways, citing privacy risks.\n\nThe company also faces legal challenges, including a privacy lawsuit in Virginia that claims its cameras amount to warrantless surveillance. Home Depot investors recently demanded reviews of the retailer's partnerships with surveillance vendors after reports that Flock's data was used in immigration enforcement. Flock has raised over $950 million from investors, valuing it at $7.5 billion.", "body_zh": "据直接了解计划的消息人士称，Flock Safety计划裁员约18%，即大约270名员工。此次裁员是在一项自愿离职买断计划之后进行的，受影响的员工将在月底离开。该公司约有1500名员工，拒绝置评。\n\nFlock总部位于亚特兰大，成立于2017年，近年来发展迅速。它在49个州提供约12万台AI摄像头和车牌识别器，服务于4800多个执法机构和近1000家企业。该公司表示其技术有助于破案。\n\n但Flock正面临监管机构、议员和隐私倡导者日益严格的审查。路透社/益普索最近的一项民调发现，38%的美国人支持在其社区使用Flock摄像头，而47%的人反对。9月，佛罗里达州以隐私风险为由，禁止在州高速公路上使用自动车牌识别器。\n\n该公司还面临法律挑战，包括弗吉尼亚州的一起隐私诉讼，指控其摄像头的大规模部署构成无证监视。家得宝的投资者最近要求审查该零售商与监控供应商的合作关系，此前有报道称Flock的数据被用于移民执法。Flock已从投资者那里筹集了超过9.5亿美元，估值达75亿美元。", "text": "Flock Safety plans to cut about 18% of its employees, or roughly 270 people, according to sources with direct knowledge of the plans. The layoffs follow a voluntary buyout program, and affected employees will leave at the end of the month. The company, which has about 1,500 workers, declined to comment. Flock, based in Atlanta and founded in 2017, has grown quickly in recent years. It provides about 120,000 AI-powered cameras and license-plate readers across 49 states, serving more than 4,800 law enforcement agencies and nearly 1,000 businesses. The company says its technology helps solve crimes. But Flock faces growing scrutiny from regulators, lawmakers, and privacy advocates. A recent Reuters/Ipsos poll found that 38% of Americans support using Flock cameras in their communities, while 47% oppose them. In September, Florida banned automated license plate readers from state highways, citing privacy risks. The company also faces legal challenges, including a privacy lawsuit in Virginia that claims its cameras amount to warrantless surveillance. Home Depot investors recently demanded reviews of the retailer's partnerships with surveillance vendors after reports that Flock's data was used in immigration enforcement. Flock has raised over $950 million from investors, valuing it at $7.5 billion.", "vocab": [{"word": "surveillance", "pos": "noun", "zh": "监视，监控", "note": "常与camera、system等搭配", "phonetic": "sә:'veilәns", "en": "close observation of a person or group (usually by the police)"}, {"word": "layoff", "pos": "noun", "zh": "裁员", "note": "常用复数layoffs", "phonetic": "'leiɒf", "en": "the act of laying off an employee or a work force"}, {"word": "backlash", "pos": "noun", "zh": "强烈反对，抵制", "note": "常与against连用", "phonetic": "'bæklæʃ", "en": "an adverse reaction to some political or social occurrence"}, {"word": "scrutiny", "pos": "noun", "zh": "审查，仔细检查", "note": "常见搭配under scrutiny", "phonetic": "'skru:tini", "en": "a prolonged intense look"}, {"word": "privacy", "pos": "noun", "zh": "隐私", "note": "privacy concerns 隐私担忧", "phonetic": "'praivәsi", "en": "the quality of being secluded from the presence or view of others"}, {"word": "advocate", "pos": "noun", "zh": "倡导者，支持者", "note": "privacy advocate 隐私倡导者", "phonetic": "'ædvәkeit", "en": "a person who pleads for a cause or propounds an idea"}, {"word": "regulator", "pos": "noun", "zh": "监管机构", "note": "常指政府监管部门", "phonetic": "'regjuleitә", "en": "any of various controls or devices for regulating or controlling fluid flow, pressure, temperature, etc."}, {"word": "lawsuit", "pos": "noun", "zh": "诉讼", "note": "file a lawsuit 提起诉讼", "phonetic": "'lɒ:sju:t", "en": "a comprehensive term for any proceeding in a court of law whereby an individual seeks a legal remedy"}, {"word": "warrantless", "pos": "adj", "zh": "无搜查令的", "note": "warrantless surveillance 无证监控", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "valuation", "pos": "noun", "zh": "估值", "note": "常与company连用", "phonetic": ".vælju'eiʃәn", "en": "assessed price"}, {"word": "partnership", "pos": "noun", "zh": "合作关系", "note": "business partnership 商业合作", "phonetic": "'pɑ:tnәʃip", "en": "the members of a business venture created by contract"}, {"word": "enforcement", "pos": "noun", "zh": "执行，执法", "note": "law enforcement 执法", "phonetic": "in'fɒ:smәnt", "en": "the act of enforcing; ensuring observance of or obedience to"}], "title_original": "AI surveillance startup Flock to cut several hundred jobs amid backlash, sources say", "summary_original": "Company, which grown rapidly in recent years, has been under scrutiny as privacy concerns grow\nFlock Safety plans to shed about 18% of its employees, people with direct ⁠knowledge of the plans said on Thursday, as the maker of\nAI\n-powered\nsurveillance\ncameras and license-plate readers faces\nmounting opposition\nto its products from communities and ⁠lawmakers.\nThe people said the job ⁠cuts came after ​a voluntary buyout program and were expected to affect roughly 270 employees at Flock, a surveillance technology startup that has seen rapid growth in recent ⁠years. Employees will leave the company at the end of the month.\nContinue reading...", "body_original": "AI surveillance startup Flock to cut several hundred jobs amid backlash, sources say\nCompany, which grown rapidly in recent years, has been under scrutiny as privacy concerns grow\nFlock Safety plans to shed about 18% of its employees, people with direct ⁠knowledge of the plans said on Thursday, as the maker of\nAI\n-powered\nsurveillance\ncameras and license-plate readers faces\nmounting opposition\nto its products from communities and ⁠lawmakers.\nThe people said the job ⁠cuts came after ​a voluntary buyout program and were expected to affect roughly 270 employees at Flock, a surveillance technology startup that has seen rapid growth in recent ⁠years. Employees will leave the company at the end of the month.\nSources declined to be identified as the company has yet to make the plan public.\nFlock declined ⁠to comment.\nIn September, Flock announced a voluntary separation program, asking employees to apply if they wanted to ​leave the company, as Wired reported. The ‌company has about 1,500 employees.\nFlock faces ‌\ngrowing scrutiny\nfrom regulators, lawmakers and privacy advocates, despite drawing strong investor interest among venture capitalists. A ‌recent Reuters/Ipsos poll showed Americans are divided on whether Flock cameras are a good idea. About 38% of the country supports the use of Flock cameras in their community, compared with 47% who oppose their use.\nAcross 49 states, Flock’s network of about 120,000 AI-powered cameras, affixed to streets and highways, automatically read license plates and record vehicles as they pass.\nThe company, based in Atlanta and founded in ‌2017, has raised more than $950m from venture capital investors, with its latest $275m round in March led by Andreessen Horowitz valuing the company at $7.5bn. The funds have supported a ​new US manufacturing facility and an upcoming push into drone products, according to the company.\nFlock provides cameras and software to more than 4,800 law enforcement agencies and nearly 1,000 businesses. The company says its technology helps investigate and solve crimes, but it has come under increasing scrutiny over alleged privacy violations and data-sharing practices.\nPublic backlash ⁠has grown. In September, Florida banned automated license plate readers from state highways, ​citing privacy risks tied to Flock’s ​cameras.\nHome Depot investors recently demanded reviews ​of the retailer’s partnerships with surveillance technology vendors after reports that Flock’s data was used ​in immigration enforcement.\nFlock also ‌faces legal challenges, such ​as a privacy ​lawsuit in Virginia alleging that the widespread deployment of its AI-powered cameras constitutes warrantless surveillance.\nTogether with AI datacenters, Flock cameras have emerged as a flashpoint ahead of November’s midterm elections, with candidates increasingly attacking their opponents by tying them to the technology.\nExplore more on these topics\nSurveillance\nAI (artificial intelligence)\nnews\nShare\nReuse this content", "fetched_at": "2026-10-10T20:57:32.679444", "timeline": [{"i": 0, "w": "Flock", "s": 0.1, "e": 0.488, "sm": 100, "em": 487, "cs": 0, "ce": 5, "si": 0}, {"i": 1, "w": "Safety", "s": 0.5, "e": 0.912, "sm": 500, "em": 912, "cs": 6, "ce": 12, "si": 0}, {"i": 2, "w": "plans", "s": 0.925, "e": 1.312, "sm": 925, "em": 1312, "cs": 13, "ce": 18, "si": 0}, {"i": 3, "w": "to", "s": 1.325, "e": 1.412, "sm": 1325, "em": 1412, "cs": 19, "ce": 21, "si": 0}, {"i": 4, "w": "cut", "s": 1.425, "e": 1.65, "sm": 1425, "em": 1650, "cs": 22, "ce": 25, "si": 0}, {"i": 5, "w": "about", "s": 1.663, "e": 1.975, "sm": 1662, "em": 1975, "cs": 26, "ce": 31, "si": 0}, {"i": 6, "w": "18", "s": 1.988, "e": 2.521, "sm": 1987, "em": 2520, "cs": 32, "ce": 34, "si": 0}, {"i": 7, "w": "%", "s": 2.521, "e": 2.788, "sm": 2520, "em": 2787, "cs": 34, "ce": 35, "si": 0}, {"i": 8, "w": "of", "s": 2.8, "e": 2.862, "sm": 2800, "em": 2862, "cs": 36, "ce": 38, "si": 0}, {"i": 9, "w": "its", "s": 2.875, "e": 3.025, "sm": 2875, "em": 3025, "cs": 39, "ce": 42, "si": 0}, {"i": 10, "w": "employees", "s": 3.038, "e